# Global Cline Rules — All Projects

These rules apply to every project regardless of technology stack or location.
Optimized for local AI model (qwen2.5-coder:14b) on RTX 3500 Ada.

---

## RULE 0 — TASK ORCHESTRATION (Default for All Large Tasks)

Use new_task by default whenever a task touches more than 3 files OR requires more than one logical concern (e.g. model + controller + service + test).

### Orchestration Workflow
1. Plan all required files and changes using Plan mode.
2. Break into subtasks:
   - One subtask per layer: Model, Migration, Service, Controller, Request, Resource, Test
   - Or one subtask per domain feature if small enough
3. Spawn each subtask with exact instructions for that subtask only.

### Subtask Instructions Must Include
- What to build (specific)
- Which files to read first
- Which files to create or edit
- Relevant conventions
- What NOT to do

---

## RULE 1 — FILE EDITING (Most Critical)

Always read before editing. Use write_to_file for files under 300 lines, replace_in_file only when block is unique and search context is exact.

### Forbidden Edit Patterns
- Never use replace_in_file without reading the file first in the same session
- No retrying failed replaces
- No chained replace_in_file calls
- No replacing reformatted code

---

## RULE 2 — FILE CREATION

1. Check if file exists before creating.
2. If it exists, read it first before editing.

Never run scaffold commands without checking target file existence.

---

## RULE 3 — TASK EXECUTION

Plan first:
1. List all files to create or modify
2. Describe changes for each file

Execute in order:
- Complete each file before moving on
- No mid-task deviations
- Fix errors immediately

Minimal footprint:
- Only touch required files
- No unnecessary commands

---

## RULE 4 — CODE QUALITY (All Languages)

- Single responsibility per function/method (max 40 lines).
- Named constants/enums for magic strings/numbers.
- Descriptive names only.
- Handle all errors explicitly.
- Validate user input before use/storage.
- Remove debug statements.

---

## RULE 5 — PHP / LARAVEL

1. Every PHP file starts with:
```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);
```

2. Architecture:
Request -> Controller (thin) -> Service -> Model
         -> Form Request -> Resource

Features to use:
- declare(strict_types=1)
- Typed properties and constructor property promotion
- Match expressions over switch
- Nullsafe operator
- Model binding

---

## RULE 6 — JAVASCRIPT / TYPESCRIPT

- Use TypeScript for all new files.
- strict: true in tsconfig.
- No `any` type, use unknown + narrowing or proper interfaces.
- const over let, never var.
- Handle all Promise rejections.
- Named exports over default exports.
- No console.log in committed code.

---

## RULE 7 — TESTING

- Write tests for every new feature and bug fix.
- Use factories for model creation.
- Name tests descriptively (e.g., "it('creates a customer with valid data')").

---

## RULE 8 — SECURITY

- Validate and sanitize all user input.
- Use parameterized queries only.
- Check authentication/authorization before protected actions.
- No sensitive data in logs.
- No wildcards in CORS for production.

---

## RULE 9 — PERFORMANCE (Local Dev Machine)

- Batch file changes into one response.
- Write complete files rather than partial replacements.
- Read only what you need.
- Don't read files multiple times unless changed.

---

## RULE 10 — COMMUNICATION

- Summarize actions before doing them.
- List files created/modified after completion.
- Explain failures with new approach.
- No debug output in committed code.